  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What Is a Long Torso?</h2>
    <p>A long torso means the upper body, from the shoulders to the hips, makes up a larger share of your height than usual, so your legs make up a smaller share. Two people can be the same height and still have very different proportions.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/long-torso-vs-long-legs-3d.webp" width="1790" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Long torso vs balanced vs long legs: 3D women and men of the same height with different leg lengths"><figcaption>Same heights, same weights: only the torso-to-leg split changes.</figcaption></figure>
    <p>Proportion researchers use the <strong>sitting-height ratio</strong> (sitting height divided by standing height). For adults it averages about 52% to 53%. A higher number means a relatively longer torso.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-tell" aria-labelledby="how-to-tell-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-tell-h">How to Tell If You Have a Long Torso</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Measure your height.</strong> Stand barefoot against a wall, heels touching it, and mark the top of your head. Measure from the floor to the mark.</li><li><strong>Measure your inseam.</strong> Stand barefoot with feet slightly apart. Measure from the crotch straight down to the floor; a book pressed up between the legs gives a level edge.</li><li><strong>Divide inseam by height.</strong> Legs are typically about 47% of height. Below about 45.5% means a long torso; above about 48.5% means long legs.</li><li><strong>Or use the finger test.</strong> Stand straight and place your fingers flat between the bottom of your rib cage and the top of your hip bone. Three or more fingers suggests a long torso; one or two, a short torso.</li></ol>
    <p><strong>Clothing clues</strong> also help: one-piece swimsuits and bodysuits that pull at the shoulders, shirts that untuck easily, and a waistline that seems low on dresses all point to a long torso. Mid-rise pants that look low-rise on you are another sign.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-h">
    <h2 id="chart-h">Long Torso Chart: What the Numbers Mean</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Leg length as a share of height (crotch to floor)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Leg share</th><th scope="col">Proportion</th><th scope="col">Typical sitting-height ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Under 45.5%</th><td>Long torso</td><td>Above 53.5%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">45.5&ndash;46%</th><td>Slightly long torso</td><td>About 53%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">46&ndash;48%</th><td>Balanced</td><td>51.5&ndash;53%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">48&ndash;48.5%</th><td>Slightly long legs</td><td>About 51%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Over 48.5%</th><td>Long legs</td><td>Below 51%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="tp-note">Thresholds are practical guides based on typical adult proportions, not medical categories. Pants inseams are usually about an inch shorter than crotch-to-floor.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="style" aria-labelledby="style-h">
    <h2 id="style-h">How to Dress a Long Torso</h2>
    <h3>For women</h3>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>High-waisted bottoms:</strong> jeans, trousers and skirts that sit at the natural waist move the visual waistline up and lengthen the legs.</li>
      <li><strong>Tuck and belt:</strong> a full or half tuck, or a belt at the narrowest point, breaks up a long upper body.</li>
      <li><strong>Cropped layers:</strong> cropped jackets, boxy cropped tops and shorter cardigans that end at the waist balance the torso.</li>
      <li><strong>Empire and fit-and-flare dresses:</strong> a seam just under the bust or at the natural waist shortens the look of the torso.</li>
      <li><strong>Long-torso sizes:</strong> many swimwear and bodysuit brands offer &ldquo;long torso&rdquo; fits that stop the straps from digging in.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>For men</h3>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li>Choose trousers with a mid or high rise and avoid very low-rise jeans.</li>
      <li>Keep shirts and jackets from running too long; a jacket that just covers the seat works best.</li>
      <li>Match belt color to trousers rather than shirt so the waistline does not cut the body short.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Your torso length combines with your shape. Check yours with the <a href="/">body shape calculator</a> or, for men, the <a href="/male-body-type-calculator/">male body type calculator</a>, then see our guides for the <a href="/hourglass-body-shape/">hourglass</a>, <a href="/pear-body-shape/">pear</a>, <a href="/apple-body-shape/">apple</a> and <a href="/inverted-triangle-body-shape/">inverted triangle</a> shapes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="short-torso" aria-labelledby="short-torso-h">
    <h2 id="short-torso-h">Long Torso vs. Short Torso</h2>
    <p>A short torso is the opposite: legs make up more than about 48.5% of height and the waist sits high. Short-torso dressing does the reverse: mid-rise rather than high-rise bottoms, untucked or longer tops, and low or no belts. Most people sit in between, and small differences are not noticeable in everyday clothing.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="fitness" aria-labelledby="fitness-h">
    <h2 id="fitness-h">Long Torso and Exercise</h2>
    <p>Limb and torso lengths change exercise mechanics. A long torso with shorter thighs usually lets you squat with a more upright back, while deadlifts can feel harder because the bar has further to travel relative to your reach. Long-legged lifters tend to lean forward more in a squat but pull well from the floor. For cycling, rowing and kayaking, torso length decides frame size and reach. To see all your proportions together, use the <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Long Torso FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do I know if I have a long torso?</h3></summary><p>Measure your inseam from the crotch to the floor and divide it by your height. Legs are typically about 47% of height. If yours are under about 45.5%, your torso is long for your height. Another quick sign: three or more fingers fit between the bottom of your ribs and the top of your hip bone.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What counts as a long torso?</h3></summary><p>A long torso means the distance from your shoulders to your hips is long compared with your legs. In numbers, it usually means a sitting height above about 53.5% of standing height or legs below about 45.5% of height.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is a long torso the same as short legs?</h3></summary><p>For a given height, yes: if your torso is longer than average, your legs are shorter than average by the same amount. People often describe the same build either way.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is it common to have a long torso?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Proportions vary a lot between people, and torso length also varies by ancestry: on average, people of East Asian descent have relatively longer torsos and people of African descent relatively longer legs, with Europeans in between.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How should I dress with a long torso?</h3></summary><p>Choose high-waisted pants and skirts, tuck in or half-tuck tops, wear belts at your natural waist, and pick cropped jackets that end at the waist. These raise the visual waistline and make the legs look longer. Look for long-torso sizes in swimsuits and bodysuits.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Can you change your torso length?</h3></summary><p>No. Torso and leg length are set by your skeleton. Posture affects how much of your height you show, and clothing can change how proportions look, but the bone lengths stay the same after growth stops.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Does a long torso affect health or fitness?</h3></summary><p>Not in itself. It can affect exercise mechanics: long-torso lifters often squat more upright and may find deadlifts harder, while long-legged lifters lean forward more in a squat. Bike and kayak fit also depend on torso length.</p></details>
  </section>

